<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Celle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Celle is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aller">Aller</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the southern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> Heath. From the late 14th century it was the residence of the Guelph dukes of Brunswick-Lüneburg ruling the Principality of Lüneburg, whose palace still stands. When the last duke, George William, died in 1705 it passed to the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>; its old town keeps hundreds of half-timbered houses.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/celle.jpg" alt="Celle" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlosscelle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Celle lies in the glacial valley of the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aller">Aller</a>, about 40 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> and 120 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>. The Lachte and the Fuhse both flow into the Aller within the town. Celle is the southern gateway to the Lüneburg Heath and, apart from Lüneburg, the largest town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a> between Hanover and Hamburg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Celle is first mentioned in a document of 985 as Kiellu, meaning fishing bay. In the 11th century it was granted the right to mint coins, some of which were found in the Sandur hoard in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroes</a>. In 1292 Duke Otto II the Strict, a Welf who ruled the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> from 1277 to 1330, left Altencelle and founded a rectangular settlement beside the existing castle, 4 kilometres to the north-west. He granted Celle town privileges in 1301 and began its town church in 1308.</p><p>From 1378 to 1705 Celle was the official residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> branch of the dukes of Brunswick-Lüneburg, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a>, who had been banished from their original ducal seat by its townsfolk. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> came in 1524, and the castle chapel, built in 1570, was consecrated in 1585. George William, the last Heath Duke, ruled from 1665 until he died in Celle Castle in 1705. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the town had only a few thousand inhabitants, and growth quickened only with industrialisation: 8,800 in 1818, 20,000 by 1900. Celle was an important garrison town in Nazi Germany, and during the pogrom of 9 and 10 November 1938 its synagogue escaped destruction only because of the risk to neighbouring buildings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Celle is the capital of its district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a> and has about 71,000 inhabitants. Its old town has more than 400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>-framed houses, among them the Hoppenerhaus of 1532, and the Old City Hall of 1561 to 1579 is known for its sandstone carvings. Schloss Celle, begun in 1530 and enlarged in baroque style in the 17th century, has a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> founded in 1674, the oldest still working in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The Bergen-Belsen concentration camp, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-frank">Anne Frank</a> died in 1945, lay in the district of Celle, and a memorial marks the site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Celle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Count Friedrich II died without heirs in 1585, Diepholz passed by feudal right to the Brunswick-Lüneburg dukes of <b>Celle</b>, and later to the Electorate of Hanover.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-diepholz">County of Diepholz</a></li><li><blockquote>The duchy lay between the Weser and the Elbe and included Hanover, <b>Celle</b> and Göttingen.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-brunswick-luneburg">Duchy of Brunswick-Lüneburg</a></li><li><blockquote>John received the northern lands around Lüneburg and <b>Celle</b>, founding the Principality of Lüneburg, while Albert took the Principality of Wolfenbüttel.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-luneburg">Principality of Lüneburg</a></li></ul></section>
